window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][33] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you tell a bad strategy from a good one? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By checking for the three parts of the kernel and for a declined option. A bad strategy is a list of goals (&quot;grow 40%, launch mobile, analytics and integrations&quot;) with no diagnosis of why the company is where it is, no approach to the main obstacle, and no statement of what it will not do. A good one names the situation, chooses a way to deal with the central challenge and lists actions that reinforce each other. My quick test is to ask what the company is deciding not to do; if the answer is nothing, it is a wish list.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What would a good version of the clinic-scheduling strategy decline? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;It would pick a focus, for example small independent clinics whose main pain is no-shows, and decline the rest for now: no large-hospital integrations, no general analytics suite, no mobile app until reminder messaging is proven. The diagnosis is that no-shows cost small clinics a large share of their revenue and that existing tools are built for big systems. The actions reinforce each other: reminders reduce no-shows, which produces the evidence for referrals, which lowers acquisition cost. The declined items are named so that the team can say no to requests.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c18-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C18-02 · Beginner · strategy · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Estimate the size of the market for a dog-walking app in a city of 1.2 million households.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> One estimate with no check.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Two routes (top-down, bottom-up), a range, and a so-what.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains the gap between routes, checks against a reality constraint, separates total from obtainable, and states the swing assumption and a way to test it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll estimate two ways and compare.</p>\n<p><em>Top-down.</em> 1,200,000 households; suppose 25% own a dog: 300,000. Say 10% use a paid walker regularly: 30,000 households. At 3 walks a week for 52 weeks at $20 a walk: 30,000 × 3 × 52 × $20 = $93.6 million a year.</p>\n<p><em>Bottom-up (supply side).</em> Suppose 2,000 active walkers in the city, each doing 6 walks a day, 5 days a week, 50 weeks a year, at $20: 2,000 × 6 × 5 × 50 × $20 = $60 million a year.</p>\n<p><em>Compare.</em> The top-down is 1.6 times the bottom-up. A likely reason: the top-down adoption (10% of owners using a walker, 3 walks a week) is optimistic, while the bottom-up counts only walkers who are currently active, and informal walkers (neighbors, friends) are not counted in either. So the current paid market is probably nearer the lower figure.</p>\n<p><em>Reality check.</em> A walker doing 6 walks a day, 5 days a week, earns $600 a week, about $30,000 a year: plausible for a full-time walker. Cross-check demand against supply: 30,000 households at 3 walks a week is 90,000 walks a week; at 30 walks per walker per week that needs 3,000 walkers, 1.5 times the 2,000 assumed. That is the same 1.5 gap, so the demand assumptions (adoption and frequency) are the likelier overstatement.</p>\n<p><em>Range and so-what.</em> $60 to $94 million for all paid walking; I'd plan on $60 million. Our obtainable share is the question: with a 5% share in three years, about $3 million in gross bookings; an app taking a 20% commission would earn about $600,000 in revenue, which may not support a team. So the size sets up the real question: what commission and share, and whether the app is more than a directory. The assumption that swings it is paid-walker adoption (10% could be 4% or 15%), which a small survey or a pilot could test.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> The two routes disagree. What do you do? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Reconcile them. Top-down gave about $94 million (30,000 households × 3 walks × 52 × $20) and supply-side gave $60 million (2,000 walkers × 6 walks × 5 days × 50 weeks × $20 is 3 million walks). The gap says something: either there are more walkers than I assumed, or the 10% of dog owners who pay regularly is too high, or walkers are working more than 6 walks a day. I would take the lower figure as a reality constraint on what can be served today and the higher as potential, and I would present the range of $60 to $94 million.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What is the obtainable share, and what is the swing assumption? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The obtainable revenue depends on how many walks we can supply. If we could capture 5% of the walks in year one, that is 150,000 walks, about $3 million at $20 a walk. The swing assumption is the share of owners who pay regularly, 10% in my top-down: halving it halves the market. I would test it with a short survey of dog owners and with a landing page that takes sign-ups for a first walk at a stated price, and I would update the estimate with the conversion rate.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c18-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C18-03 · Intermediate · strategy · ST · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Should a device maker launch a subscription bundle of its services?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, bundles increase revenue,&quot; or &quot;no, it will cannibalize.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Diagnoses the situation, models the economics, and addresses cannibalization.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Names ecosystem integration as the source of advantage, computes the break-even on cannibalization and the retention benefit, states the assumption to test, and proposes a staged test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general: I know the product only from public information, and nothing here describes any real plan. I'd first ask what the company wants (more revenue per customer, lower churn, a stronger ecosystem) and diagnose the current state: how many customers pay for how many services.</p>\n<p><em>Diagnosis (invented).</em> Per 1,000 users: 250 pay for two or more services (average $22 a month), 450 pay for one ($8), and 300 pay for none. A bundle of four services at $20 is cheaper than buying them separately.</p>\n<p><em>Where the advantage comes from.</em> Ecosystem integration: the services work better together and with the device, and customers who use several are less likely to leave. A bundle reinforces that and makes switching costlier (C18.5).</p>\n<p><em>Economics.</em> Three effects. <em>Cannibalization:</em> the 250 multi-service payers move to $20 and lose $2 each: −$500 a month per 1,000 users. <em>Upgrades:</em> single-service payers who add the others at +$12 each; suppose 5% do: 450 × 0.05 × $12 = +$270. <em>New payers:</em> non-payers who join at $20; suppose 3%: 300 × 0.03 × $20 = +$180. Net: −$500 + $270 + $180 = −$50 a month per 1,000 users: roughly break-even on the first month. With 8% upgrades and 4% new payers: +$172. So on revenue alone the bundle is marginal; its value depends on adoption.</p>\n<p><em>The retention benefit.</em> If bundle subscribers churn less, say 2.1% a month against 3.0% (invented), the expected lifetime rises from 33 months to 48 months. For a multi-service payer, $22 a month over 33 months is $733; $20 over 48 months is $952: a gain of about $219 per customer in lifetime revenue, in spite of the lower monthly price. So the bundle pays if the retention effect is real, and it does not if it is not. That is the assumption to test.</p>\n<p><em>Other effects.</em> Pricing power: a bundle makes it harder to raise individual prices; each service's team may lose visibility of its own economics; regulators may examine bundles that tie services together <code>[V: the European Commission's 2022 decision on Amazon's Prime and Buy Box practices is an example]</code>.</p>\n<p><em>Recommendation.</em> Test it before committing: offer the bundle in one market for a quarter, with a holdout, and measure upgrades, new payers and, most importantly, retention of bundle subscribers against a matched group. Launch more widely if retention improves by enough to give a positive lifetime value (at least a 20% longer lifetime in this example). I would change my mind if the retention gain were small, in which case the bundle is a discount.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that strategy prompts at Apple include ecosystem and services decisions <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real bundle, price or data.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you compute the break-even on cannibalization? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With round numbers of my own, labeled invented. Suppose 250 users per 1,000 pay $22 a month for two services separately, $5,500. A bundle of those services at $18 would, if all 250 switched, bring $4,500, a loss of $1,000. To make up for it the bundle needs about 1,000 ÷ 18 = 56 extra subscribers, a rise of 22%, or a retention gain that is worth the same. If the bundle also reduces monthly churn for bundled users, that adds value over time. The break-even therefore depends on how many new subscribers and how much retention improvement the bundle brings, and that is the assumption to test.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What staged test would you run? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Offer the bundle to a random 10% of eligible customers in one country for eight weeks, with a control group that sees the existing offers. Measure take-up, the share who switch from separate subscriptions (cannibalization), new subscribers, churn over the period and revenue per user. Decide in advance: continue if net revenue per eligible user is higher than the control's or churn falls enough to pay back in a year; stop if the net is lower and churn is unchanged. The integration across devices is the source of advantage, so I would also measure how many services bundle users actually use.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c18-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C18-04 · Intermediate · strategy · ST · Google</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A company has a popular free, ad-supported consumer product. Should it launch a paid, ad-free tier?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, subscriptions are good revenue.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Computes the revenue gained against ad revenue lost, and notes who would pay.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Recognizes selection (heavy users are worth more in ads), sets the break-even, considers the effects on the free product, positioning and trust, and proposes a test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; I know such products only from public information, and nothing here describes a real plan. The crux: the people who would pay to remove ads are the people the ads earn the most from.</p>\n<p><em>Numbers (invented).</em> 1,000,000 monthly users; average ad revenue $2.40 per user per month. A paid tier at $8. Suppose 4% convert: 40,000 payers, $320,000 a month. If the converters were average users, the ad revenue lost is 40,000 × $2.40 = $96,000, a net of +$224,000. But paying users are typically heavier users: if their ad value is $5 a month, the loss is $200,000, a net of +$120,000. The break-even is where the converters' ad value equals the price: if they are worth $8 a month in ads, the tier gains nothing. So the key estimate is the ad value of the converters, which I can measure from usage.</p>\n<p><em>Other effects.</em> Does the paid tier change the free product (fewer heavy users sharing content, a weaker network)? Does it affect advertisers (a smaller audience)? Does it change how users see the company (a paid product has to be better; trust is higher when the customer pays)? Is there a segment (professionals) that would pay for features beyond ad removal, which would justify the price better?</p>\n<p><em>Alternatives.</em> A lower-priced tier; feature-based paid tiers; a usage limit on the free tier. I'd evaluate the paid tier on the heavy segments first.</p>\n<p><em>Recommendation.</em> Run a limited test: offer the paid tier to a sample of heavy users, measure conversion, the ad revenue they leave, retention and any effect on others, and compute the net per converter. Launch if the net per converter is clearly positive and retention is higher among payers. What would change my mind: converters' ad value near $8.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that strategy prompts at Google concern monetizing free consumer products <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or plan is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you set the break-even given who converts? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The people who pay to remove ads are the heavy users, who earn the most in ads. If converters average $6 of ad revenue a month, not the $2.40 average, a $8 price gains only $2 per converter, and 40,000 payers add about $80,000 a month net, not $320,000. The break-even price is the ad revenue the converters used to generate, here $6. I would estimate converters' ad revenue from the data on heavy users and check that the net is positive after payment costs and support.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What are the effects on the free product, positioning and trust? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Heavy users leaving the free tier lowers total ad impressions and may reduce advertisers' reach, so I would check advertiser spend. Positioning: a paid ad-free tier tells users the ads are a cost, which could make the free experience feel worse. Trust: if the free product's ad load rises to make up the revenue, free users lose. I would test in one market for eight weeks, measuring total revenue per user across both tiers, free-user engagement and advertiser reach, and keep the free tier's ad load unchanged during the test.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c18-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C18-05 · Hard · strategy · ST · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How should a platform owner respond if regulation opens alternative app distribution?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Fight it,&quot; or &quot;comply and accept lower revenue.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies what is at stake (revenue, security, quality, developers, users) and lays out response options.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Models revenue under different shifts, separates what regulation requires from what the owner chooses, considers second-order effects on security and developers, and proposes a staged response with measures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> This is an Illustrative scenario, with regulatory facts as of 2026-10 <code>[V: Apple Form 10-Q, 2026, on US and EU proceedings; the EU Digital Markets Act]</code>; I know the area only from public information, and nothing here describes any real company's plans. Several jurisdictions have adopted or considered rules that require large platforms to allow other app stores or payment methods. I'd treat the question as how to respond, in order.</p>\n<p><em>Diagnosis.</em> What is at stake: (1) <em>revenue</em>: the platform earns a commission on developer billings; (2) <em>security and quality</em>: the platform's review and distribution control is part of its promise to users; (3) <em>developers</em>: they want lower fees and direct relationships; (4) <em>users</em>: they value safety, simplicity and choice; (5) <em>the ecosystem advantage</em>: integration between devices and services (C18.5).</p>\n<p><em>Revenue under shifts (illustrative index).</em> Developers bill 100 through the platform; commission 30%: revenue 30. Suppose regulation allows alternatives, and the platform charges a reduced technology fee of 5% on billings that move. If 10% of billings move: 90 × 30% + 10 × 5% = 27 + 0.5 = 27.5, down 8%. If 30% move: 70 × 30% + 30 × 5% = 21 + 1.5 = 22.5, down 25%. The size of the shift is the swing assumption, and it depends on how much developers gain by moving and how much users will follow.</p>\n<p><em>Options.</em> (A) Contest the rule and comply minimally. (B) Comply fully and compete on value: invest in the quality of the platform's own store and services so that users and developers choose it. (C) Comply and redesign the commercial terms (a lower commission with a fee for services such as payments, review, discovery, and updates) so that the platform's revenue tracks the value it provides. (D) Comply with strong safeguards (verification, notarization, clear warnings) for alternative channels.</p>\n<p><em>Evaluation.</em> A delays and risks penalties and goodwill. B and C aim to keep users and developers on the platform by merit. D protects users from the new risks (malware, scams) that alternative channels can create, a real second-order effect. The options combine: C and D are compatible, and B is the long-term goal.</p>\n<p><em>Second-order effects.</em> Security incidents on alternative channels could harm users and the platform's reputation even if the platform is not at fault; developers may fragment their releases; competitors may exploit the opening; privacy promises may be harder to keep. Regulators may scrutinize the new terms themselves.</p>\n<p><em>Recommendation.</em> Comply in good faith, restructure commercial terms to charge for value (C), build the safeguards (D), and invest in making the platform's store the best place to distribute (B). Measure: share of billings that move, security incident rate, developer satisfaction, user trust, and revenue per user. Stage by region where rules apply. I'd change my mind if the share of billings moving were small, which would mean less urgency, or large, which would call for a deeper rethink of the model.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only (a platform owner of the kind Apple and Google are): <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that strategy prompts at Apple include platform and regulatory scenarios <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any company's actual response, fees or rules.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What does regulation require and what does the owner choose? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would separate the two carefully. The requirement is whatever the rule states for the jurisdiction, for example permitting alternative stores or payment methods, and I would confirm the exact text with legal <code>[V: EU Digital Markets Act; Apple Form 10-Q on app-store proceedings]</code>. The choices are everything the rule leaves open: fees on alternative distribution, how security review works, how users are warned, and how developers are supported. The response should comply fully, choose terms that are lawful and defensible, and avoid actions that read as obstruction. Treating the choices as a business decision, with legal review, keeps the response honest.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you model revenue under different shifts? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With scenarios. Suppose commission revenue is 100 on a base where the platform takes 30% of spend. If 15% of spend moves to an alternative channel where the platform charges a 10% fee on the moved spend, revenue is 85 × 0.30 + 15 × 0.10 = 27.0 on the same spend base (index), about 10% below the 30 on that base. At a 30% shift it is 70 × 0.30 + 30 × 0.10 = 24.0, a 20% fall. I would show 5%, 15% and 30%, say which assumptions drive them (the fee and the share that moves), and set measures: share of spend moved, developer retention and security incidents.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c18-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C18-06 · Hard · strategy · ST · Microsoft</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> An enterprise software company has three business lines: a mature suite that funds the company, a fast-growing platform, and an experimental line. How do you allocate investment?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Put the most money in the growing business.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Assigns each line a role and criteria, and allocates with reasons.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Protects the cash source, sets stage gates for the experiment, uses numbers on return, names interdependencies between lines, and states what would change the allocation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; I know such companies only from public information. The first move is to give each line a role, because the right investment depends on what the line is for.</p>\n<p><em>Roles (invented figures).</em> The <em>mature suite</em>: revenue 60 (index), growth 3% a year, margin 45%. Its role is to fund the company and to defend against erosion. The <em>growth platform</em>: revenue 30, growth 25%, margin 30%. Its role is to become the next major business and to be the company's future. The <em>experimental line</em>: revenue 2, growth 100%, margin deeply negative. Its role is to find out whether a new bet is worth scaling.</p>\n<p><em>Criteria.</em> Return on the strategic goal, size of the opportunity, strength of the advantage, risk, and interdependence (does one line strengthen another?). For example, the platform may sell to the suite's customers, and the suite's relationships may be its distribution.</p>\n<p><em>Allocation logic.</em> Suppose 10 units of investment capacity. <em>Suite:</em> enough to defend and modernize, since it produces 27 of profit (60 × 45%) and any erosion hurts everything; say 2. <em>Platform:</em> the largest share, since growth at 25% on 30 is 7.5 of new revenue a year, with a good chance of becoming the largest line; say 6. <em>Experiment:</em> a bounded amount with stage gates, say 2, released in stages against milestones (customer evidence, unit economics) with a stop rule.</p>\n<p><em>Numbers to check.</em> The platform's growth adds 7.5 in revenue at 30% margin, about 2.25 of profit a year at current margins, rising as scale grows; the experiment may have a low chance of success but a high payoff. The suite's decline rate matters: if it fell by 5 a year, that would be worth more than a small platform gain, so I'd check what the suite needs to hold its position.</p>\n<p><em>Interdependencies.</em> If the platform's customers come through the suite's relationships, cutting suite investment harms the platform. I'd map such dependencies.</p>\n<p><em>Decision and review.</em> Allocate about 2, 6 and 2, review each quarter, and shift if the platform's growth slows, if the suite shows faster erosion or if the experiment reaches a gate. What would change my mind: evidence that the experiment is more promising than its stage suggests (a larger share), or that the suite's customers are at risk of leaving (more to defend).</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Microsoft is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that portfolio-allocation prompts at Microsoft involve mature, growth and experimental lines <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Microsoft data or plan is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What stage gates would you set for the experimental line? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Fund in tranches tied to evidence, with a cap. For example, a budget of no more than 5% of operating income; at month six, 30% weekly retention among pilot customers; at month twelve, at least five paying customers each above $50,000 a year; and miss a gate and the line is stopped or reshaped. Each gate is written in advance and reviewed by someone outside the experimental team. This lets the company explore without letting the experiment grow into a drain on the lines that fund it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you protect the cash source, and what would change the allocation? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The mature suite keeps the investment needed for reliability, security and defense against erosion, since it funds the company; cutting it to feed growth would shrink the source. I would name the interdependencies: the platform sells into the suite's customers, so suite churn hurts the platform, and the experimental line may use shared services. The allocation would change if platform growth falls below, say, 15%, if suite churn rises, or if the experiment hits a gate early. I would review the allocation every six months against these triggers.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c18-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C18-07 · Hard · strategy · ST · Amazon</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A large online marketplace is considering selling its own branded products in categories where third-party sellers do well. Should it?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, own brands have higher margins,&quot; or &quot;no, it will upset sellers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs margin against seller trust and regulatory risk, with numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Models the net including diverted commission and trust effects, identifies the mechanism of the marketplace advantage (selection, trust), proposes safeguards, and states a test and conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; I know the topic only from public information, and nothing here describes any real company's practice. Marketplaces and their owners' own products have been the subject of public scrutiny and regulatory attention in several jurisdictions <code>[V: European Commission decision of 20 December 2022 on Amazon's use of seller data and Buy Box; Digital Markets Act self-preferencing rule]</code>, which is part of the risk.</p>\n<p><em>Diagnosis.</em> A marketplace's advantage is selection and trust: buyers come because it has what they want and they can rely on the process; sellers come because buyers are there (a network effect, C18.5). The question is whether competing with sellers erodes that advantage.</p>\n<p><em>Numbers (illustrative).</em> A category has gross merchandise value of $1 billion. The marketplace earns a 15% commission: $150 million. Suppose its own brand captures 10% of the category ($100 million of sales) at a 35% margin: $35 million. But those sales come largely from sellers' sales, so the commission on them is lost: 15% × $100 million = $15 million. Net: +$20 million.</p>\n<p>Now suppose sellers respond: a few leave and others raise prices, and the category's total sales fall 3% ($30 million), costing $4.5 million of commission. Net: +$15.5 million. If the response is worse (a 10% fall, $100 million, $15 million of commission), the net is +$5 million and the long-run damage could exceed it. The estimate of the seller and buyer response is the swing assumption.</p>\n<p><em>Beyond the arithmetic.</em> Trust has a multiplier: sellers make decisions about where to invest based on whether the platform will use their success against them. Concerns include use of sellers' sales data to choose what to copy, favored placement for own products, and the effect on buyer trust if results look biased. Regulation can change the economics overnight.</p>\n<p><em>Options.</em> (A) No own brands. (B) Own brands only where sellers are thin (gaps in selection), which fills selection and competes less directly. (C) Own brands with strict safeguards: no use of individual sellers' data, clear labeling, no preferential ranking, and independent audit. (D) Free entry in all categories.</p>\n<p><em>Recommendation.</em> B with C's safeguards: enter where selection or quality is poor, which expands the marketplace's value, and keep firm rules on data and ranking. Measure: seller retention and investment, buyer trust, category growth, and the diverted-commission arithmetic, with a stop rule if seller exits exceed a threshold. What would change my mind: evidence that sellers in the category are leaving or that regulators are acting, which would favor A.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Amazon is the setting only (a large marketplace operator): <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that marketplace-conflict prompts appear at Amazon <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about Amazon's actual practices, data or decisions.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you model the net effect including diverted commission and trust? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With three effects, using invented figures. Suppose own-brand sells 1,000 units a day at $6 margin: $6,000. If 600 of those units were diverted from third-party sellers who would have paid $3 each in commission, the platform loses $1,800, for a net of $4,200 a day before trust effects. Now the trust effect: if buyers or sellers believe the platform favors its own products, and total marketplace sales fall by 0.5% on $5 million of daily sales at a 15% commission, the loss is about $3,750 a day, which nearly erases the gain. So the decision turns on the trust effect, which I would measure by seller retention and buyer conversion.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What safeguards would you propose? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Structural ones. Do not use non-public seller data to design or price own-brand products; do not give own-brand products preference in search or placement, and label them clearly; keep teams separate with documented rules; and have an independent review of ranking. Measures: third-party seller retention and revenue in the categories, the share of search results that are own-brand at equal quality, and complaints. Because regulators in several places have examined this question <code>[V: European Commission decision of 20 December 2022; Digital Markets Act]</code>, I would involve legal early and treat the safeguards as the price of doing it at all.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c18-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C18-08 · Hard · strategy · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Your productivity-software company wants to add voice transcription to its notes product. Should it build the capability, buy a company that has it, or partner with a provider?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Build it, because we should own our technology,&quot; or &quot;partner, because it's faster.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares the options on cost, time and control, and says whether the capability is core.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Decides by whether the capability differentiates the product, models the volume at which build overtakes partner, prices the delay as well as the spend, protects against dependence (data, terms, exit), and says what would change the choice.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>Start with the question that decides the rest (the chooser is FW-C18-07).</em> Is transcription something customers choose us for, or something they expect as a basic part of notes? If it is a basic expectation, speed and cost matter most and ownership matters least; if it is where we want to win (for example, a unique accuracy on meetings), control and learning matter more.</p>\n<p><em>Options and figures (all invented).</em> Partner: license a provider at $0.012 a minute with about six weeks to integrate. Build: about $3.0 million and twelve months, with a run cost of $0.004 a minute. Buy: a company at about $25 million with a team of twenty engineers and a small user base. At 10 million minutes a month, partner costs 0.012 × 10,000,000 × 12 = $1.44 million a year and build costs $0.48 million a year to run, so build's extra $3.0 million is repaid in 3.0 ÷ (1.44 − 0.48) = about 3.1 years. At 30 million minutes a month, partner costs $4.32 million a year against $1.44 million, and build is repaid in about one year. So volume decides when building pays.</p>\n<p><em>Delay has a price.</em> Building takes twelve months against six weeks: if the feature is expected to lift retention or win deals, those twelve months of lost benefit belong in the comparison, as does the risk that a competitor ships first.</p>\n<p><em>Risks of each.</em> Partner: dependence on a provider's price and quality, and where our customers' audio goes (privacy and data terms). Build: delivery risk and distraction from the roadmap. Buy: integration risk and paying for a team and product we may not need; it makes sense only when the capability and the team are both scarce and strategic.</p>\n<p><em>Recommendation.</em> Partner now, to ship in weeks and learn how customers use it, with contract terms that protect data, cap price increases and allow an exit; revisit building when volume passes about 25 million minutes a month or when accuracy for our use case becomes a reason customers choose us. Buy only if a partner cannot meet the quality bar and a team with the capability is available at a price that is small beside the value. I'd measure adoption, accuracy on our own audio, cost per minute and customer privacy concerns, and I'd change my view if the provider's terms worsened or our volume grew faster than expected.</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> The core-or-context question; a computed break-even on volume; the price of delay; dependence risks; a staged recommendation with triggers.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you decide whether to acquire a company instead? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd ask whether we need the technology, the team or the customers, and whether we could get it more cheaply by partnering or hiring. An acquisition is justified when the capability is strategic, the team is scarce, and buying is faster than building by enough to matter, and when we can integrate it without losing the people. At $25 million, I'd compare it with the build cost of $3 million and the partner cost over several years; unless the team is the real prize, it is the most expensive route. I'd also run diligence on quality, security and the retention of key people, and I'd set integration criteria before signing.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the partner raises prices or changes its terms? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;That is the main risk of partnering, so I'd plan for it up front: a price cap or notice period in the contract, a second provider qualified in parallel if the feature matters, and an abstraction in our product so that switching is a weeks-long task, not a rewrite. I'd also track cost per minute and the point at which building becomes cheaper, so that a price increase moves the decision rather than surprising us.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c18-09\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C18-09 · Hard · strategy · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What should the product vision be for a mid-sized photo-sharing app over the next three years, and how would you turn it into a roadmap?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A list of features: &quot;AI editing, video, a marketplace.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States who the product is for, the change it makes in their lives, and a few strategic bets, ordered in time.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Grounds the vision in user and market trends and the company's advantages, states what it will not do, converts it into outcomes and bets by horizon with metrics and kill criteria, and says how it would learn that the vision is wrong.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>Ground it.</em> A vision should answer who we serve, what changes for them, and why we can win. I'd start from evidence: how people share photos now (messaging apps and private groups, more than public feeds), what our users do that is distinctive (suppose families sharing within small circles), and what we do better than large platforms (privacy, a calm experience, long-term storage and organization). I'd also name the trends that could hurt us: sharing moving to messaging, and phone makers building photo features into the device.</p>\n<p><em>A vision statement.</em> &quot;The place where a family's photos live and are shared with the people who matter, for decades.&quot; It is specific about the user (families and close circles), the job (keep and share), and the quality (private and lasting). It leaves out public fame-seeking and ad-driven feeds, which is a strategic choice: what we will not do.</p>\n<p><em>From vision to outcomes by horizon.</em> Now (next two quarters): make sharing within circles effortless, measured by shares per active family and the share of new users who invite a second person within a week (say from 28% to 40%). Next (quarters three to six): make the archive worth keeping, through search by person and event and annual books, measured by weekly retrievals per user and 12-month retention. Later (year two and three): extend to shared family history and relatives who are not users, measured by non-user recipients who become users and by paid conversion. For each bet I'd write the assumption, a small test and a kill criterion (C26), so that the roadmap is a set of testable bets, not promised features.</p>\n<p><em>Guardrails and honesty.</em> The roadmap should commit to outcomes and ranges, and show confidence falling with distance: high for now, medium for next, low for later. I'd keep capacity for the unplanned and review the vision yearly against what the data says.</p>\n<p><em>How I'd know it's wrong.</em> Leading indicators: if circles do not grow, if retrieval stays low, or if shares move to messaging apps faster than we can integrate. I'd set a review date and thresholds in advance, and be ready to change the target user, not just the features.</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Evidence-based user and trend grounding; a specific vision with exclusions; outcomes by horizon with metrics; bets with kill criteria; how it could be wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you keep a three-year vision from becoming a promise to stakeholders? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By separating the vision from the roadmap and showing confidence by horizon. The vision is a direction and a choice about who we serve; the roadmap commits to outcomes for the next two quarters and shows later items as bets with their assumptions. When someone asks for a date on a later item, I give the test that would earn it a date. I also review the plan quarterly and say publicly what changed and why, so changes read as learning.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What would you not do, and why does it matter? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A vision is only useful if it excludes things. Here I would not build a public, ad-funded feed, because it would pull the product toward attention and away from privacy and the family use case, and it would put us against much larger platforms on their strengths. Saying this early helps me say no to attractive requests, and it makes the roadmap coherent.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c18-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are assumptions unless marked, and the arithmetic was computed.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c18-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C18-01 · Turn a goal into a strategy</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Rewrite each as diagnosis, guiding policy and coherent actions, naming one thing declined: (a) &quot;Grow the app's weekly users 50%.&quot; (b) &quot;Be the number one meal-kit brand.&quot; (c) &quot;Expand internationally.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Three slides from a planning deck.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Invent plausible diagnoses and mark them <code>(invented)</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C18-01 and the detector (FW-C18-08).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) <em>Diagnosis:</em> 60% of weekly users arrive from one referral channel and half of new users leave in a week because the first session is empty (invented). <em>Policy:</em> grow by fixing activation and deepening the referral loop, not by buying traffic. <em>Actions:</em> a guided first session, an invite flow, a weekly digest. <em>Declined:</em> paid acquisition this year. (b) <em>Diagnosis:</em> the category is crowded and customers churn on price; our edge is dietary-specific kits (invented). <em>Policy:</em> be the best for people with dietary needs.</p>\n<p><em>Actions:</em> specialized recipes, a nutritionist partnership, a retention program. <em>Declined:</em> the general family market. (c) <em>Diagnosis:</em> 40% of our customers already ship to one neighboring country at high cost (invented). <em>Policy:</em> enter that country first through a partner. <em>Actions:</em> local payment methods, a partner for logistics. <em>Declined:</em> other countries until unit economics are proven.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a measure for each policy that tests whether it is working.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A focused strategy gives up opportunities; naming what is declined is the discipline.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c18-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C18-02 · Size a market two ways</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Size the annual market for a car-wash subscription in a city of 800,000 households. Top-down: 70% of households own a car, 6% subscribe, at $300 a year. Bottom-up: 60 car-wash sites, 700 members each, at $300 a year. Compute both, the gap and a planning range, and name the swing assumption.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A market-entry case.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C18-03.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Top-down: 800,000 × 0.70 = 560,000 cars; × 0.06 = 33,600 subscribers; × $300 = $10.08 million. Bottom-up: 60 × 700 = 42,000 members × $300 = $12.6 million. The bottom-up is 1.25 times the top-down, close, which is reassuring. A planning range of $10 million to $13 million. Reality check: 42,000 members is 7.5% of cars, so the subscription rate would need to be near 7.5%; the 6% assumption is below that, and the sites may serve people from outside the city. The swing assumption is the subscription rate (6% could be 3% or 10%). A pilot or a survey of members at existing sites would test it.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Cross-check with the usage of the nearest alternative (pay-per-wash revenue per site).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Two routes that agree can share a hidden assumption; check the reality constraint.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c18-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C18-03 · Unit economics with sensitivities</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A subscription costs $10 a month; variable cost is $3.50; CAC is $45; monthly churn is 6%. Compute contribution, payback, lifetime, lifetime contribution and the ratio to CAC. Then recompute for churn 9%, and for churn 9% with CAC $70.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A pricing and growth review.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C18-06.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Contribution $6.50 a month. Payback $45 ÷ $6.50 = 6.9 months. Lifetime 1 ÷ 0.06 = 16.7 months; lifetime contribution $108; ratio 2.4. At 9% churn: lifetime 11.1 months, lifetime contribution $72, ratio 1.6. At 9% churn and CAC $70: payback 10.8 months, ratio 1.03 (barely covering acquisition). The business is healthy at 6% churn and fragile if churn and acquisition cost worsen together. The strategic implication: retention is the first lever, and a channel with cheaper acquisition the second.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Raise the price to $12 with the same costs: contribution $8.50, payback 5.3 months at CAC $45, and test the effect on churn.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Higher price raises margin and may raise churn; measure both.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c18-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C18-04 · Test the claimed advantage</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For each claim, say whether the mechanism exists, whether it is durable, and what would erode it: (a) &quot;Our photo app has network effects because we have many users.&quot; (b) &quot;Our data is our moat.&quot; (c) &quot;Customers can't leave because their documents are stored with us.&quot; (d) &quot;We have scale: we are the biggest.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A pitch deck.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C18-05.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Probably no mechanism: many users does not mean each user's value rises with others; unless people share albums so that value depends on friends being there, it is scale, not a network effect. (b) Depends: data is a moat only if more data keeps improving the product and rivals cannot get similar data; many data advantages saturate. (c) A switching cost: real if export is hard; durable only until export tools or regulation lower the cost, and customers resent lock-in. (d) Scale is an advantage only if it lowers cost per unit or improves the product; being biggest is a position, not a mechanism. In each case ask what the leader can do that a smaller rival cannot.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Ask what evidence would show the mechanism (churn is lower among users with many connections).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A strict test rejects many claims; that is its purpose.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c18-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C18-05 · Choose positioning axes</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For a project-management tool, choose two axes from the customer's point of view that would separate the main alternatives (a spreadsheet, a simple task app, a full enterprise suite, a developer-focused tool), place them, and name a gap.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A positioning discussion.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C18-04.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Axes: setup effort (minutes to useful) against depth (how much complexity it can handle). Spreadsheet: low setup, low depth. Simple task app: low setup, medium depth. Enterprise suite: high setup, high depth. Developer tool: medium setup, high depth for one type of team. The gap: low setup with high depth for non-technical teams (a product that starts simple and grows with the team). The risk: that is where a simple tool can expand upward and where an enterprise suite can come down; check what each cannot do without hurting its main business (an enterprise suite cannot become simple without confusing its buyers).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Use price against service level.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Axes chosen after the answer flatter it; choose from customer interviews.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c18-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C18-06 · Build, buy or partner over two horizons</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A capability would add $15 million a year of revenue at a 60% contribution margin once shipped. Build: 14 months, $8 million. Buy: 8 months, $30 million. Partner: 4 months, vendor takes 30% of revenue. Compute the net over three years and over five years for each, and recommend.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A capability gap.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Ignore maintenance and discounting.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C18-07.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Three years: build (36 − 14) ÷ 12 × $15M = $27.5M revenue × 0.60 = $16.5M less $8M = $8.5M. Buy: (36 − 8) ÷ 12 × $15M = $35M × 0.60 = $21M less $30M = −$9M. Partner: (36 − 4) ÷ 12 × $15M = $40M × (0.60 − 0.30) = $12M. Five years: build (60 − 14) ÷ 12 × $15M = $57.5M × 0.60 = $34.5M less $8M = $26.5M. Buy: 52 ÷ 12 × $15M = $65M × 0.60 = $39M less $30M = $9M. Partner: 56 ÷ 12 × $15M = $70M × 0.30 = $21M. Partner leads at three years ($12M against $8.5M), build leads at five ($26.5M against $21M); buy trails unless the acquired assets have other value. Recommend partnering now with a plan to build, if the capability is core and the five-year view is the relevant one.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a discount rate; the ranking at three years moves further toward partner.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Running a partner and a build in parallel costs more and keeps the early revenue.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c18-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C18-07 · The break-even for matching a free tier</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A product has 50,000 payers at $12. A rival's free tier prompts a decision to launch one: 200,000 free users, 3% convert to $12, ads earn $0.40 per free user a month. What share of current payers can downgrade before matching loses money?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A competitive response.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Gains per month: conversions 200,000 × 0.03 × $12 = $72,000; ads 200,000 × $0.40 = $80,000; total $152,000. Each downgrade costs $12. Break-even downgrades: $152,000 ÷ $12 = 12,667, which is 12,667 ÷ 50,000 = 25.3% of payers. If more than about a quarter of payers would downgrade, matching loses money. Test the downgrade rate before launching broadly.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Design the free tier so heavy-use features are absent, to reduce the downgrade rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A constrained free tier attracts fewer users and cannibalizes less.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c18-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C18-08 · Spot the strategy theater</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For each statement say what is wrong and how to fix it: (a) &quot;Our strategy is to leverage synergies to deliver best-in-class customer value.&quot; (b) &quot;Our strategy: increase revenue 30%, reduce churn 20%, launch five features.&quot; (c) &quot;We will do everything: grow in all segments and all regions.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A planning review.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C18-08.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Fluff: nothing could be wrong with it; replace it with a specific claim, such as &quot;win teams of under ten by being usable in an hour.&quot; (b) Goals and a project list, no diagnosis or approach; add the diagnosis (why churn is high) and the policy. (c) No tradeoffs: nothing is declined, so it is not a choice; name the segment and region to win first and what is deferred.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Apply the question &quot;what will we not do?&quot; to each.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A sharper statement can be wrong, which is why it is useful.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c18-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C18-09 · Repair an OKR</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Fix this OKR: <em>Objective: Make onboarding great. KR1: Launch the new tutorial. KR2: Complete the redesign. KR3: Run five user interviews.</em> Write an objective and three key results that are outcomes, with targets.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A quarterly goal-setting.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Invent baselines and mark them <code>(invented)</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C18.8.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Objective:</em> New users reach their first success quickly and come back. <em>KR1:</em> Raise the share of new users who complete the first task within 10 minutes from 38% to 55% (invented). <em>KR2:</em> Raise day-7 retention of new users from 24% to 30%. <em>KR3:</em> Cut onboarding-related support contacts per 100 new users from 6 to 4. The original key results were outputs (launch, complete, interview); the revised ones measure the outcome the work should produce. Add a guardrail (sign-up completion must not fall) and agree that the key results are for learning and not for rating individuals.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Cut to two key results to keep focus.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Outcome targets can be missed for reasons beyond the team; review honestly.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c18-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C18-10 · A timed full strategy answer</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Give a 25-minute answer to: &quot;Should a ride-hailing company enter food delivery?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Self-scored; record yourself. State assumptions aloud.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None. Label what you assume.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Use the 30-minute budget in section 4, compressed.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The strategy spine (FW-C18-02).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> An outline of a strong answer. Objective: more usage per customer and better use of drivers. Diagnose: customers (frequent, time-sensitive), market (large, contested, low margin), competition (specialists with restaurant relationships; their advantage is supply density and restaurant ties), the company's advantages (an existing user base, a fleet of drivers, payments, routing). Sizing with two routes. Unit economics: order value, commission, courier cost per order, density (drops per hour), CAC (low, since it can market to existing riders); sensitivity to density.</p>\n<p>Options: enter fully, enter in cities where the fleet is dense, partner with an incumbent, or not enter. Second-order effects: restaurants' trust, drivers' earnings during idle time (an upside), brand safety, cannibalization of ride supply at peak. Recommendation: pilot in two dense cities, use idle driver time, with thresholds (drops per hour, order value, retention) and a stop rule. What would change my mind: low density, or an incumbent with a strong lock on restaurants.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Partner with a delivery specialist for restaurant supply and bring the user base.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Entering adjacent markets uses existing assets and spreads attention.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for four weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: choices and sizing.</em> Use IQ-C18-01, IQ-C18-02, EX-C18-01, EX-C18-02 and EX-C18-08. Rewrite goals as strategy, size two ways, and spot theater.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: advantage and economics.</em> Use IQ-C18-03, IQ-C18-04, EX-C18-03, EX-C18-04, EX-C18-05 and EX-C18-07. Compute unit economics and break-evens by hand, and test advantage claims.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: decisions with structure.</em> Use IQ-C18-05 to IQ-C18-09, EX-C18-06 and EX-C18-09. Practice platform, portfolio and marketplace cases, build-buy-partner over two horizons, and OKR repair.</li>\n<li><em>Week 4: full cases.</em> Use EX-C18-10 and two prompts you have not seen. Run 25-minute answers with a partner who changes the objective midway.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to say &quot;What's your point of view?&quot;, &quot;How do you know the market is that big?&quot; and &quot;What will they do in response?&quot; at random. Practice committing to a recommendation in two sentences.</p>\n<p><strong>Read one real strategy.</strong> Read a public strategy memo or shareholder letter of a company you follow, and write its diagnosis, policy and actions in three sentences, and what it declines.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I state the objective and diagnose before proposing?</li>\n<li>Did I size the market with two routes and a reality check?</li>\n<li>Did I analyze competition by customer value, including substitutes and responses?</li>\n<li>Did I name and test the source of advantage?</li>\n<li>Did I compute unit economics with sensitivities and net out cannibalization?</li>\n<li>Were my options different in kind, and compared over the right horizon?</li>\n<li>Did I commit to a recommendation, sequence it and say what is declined?</li>\n<li>Did I say what would change my view?</li>\n<li>Did I avoid recited frameworks, goals as strategy and roadmap as strategy?</li>\n<li>Did I finish with the two-sentence close from section 4?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the prompt, the diagnosis, the recommendation, the key number and assumption, your score out of ten and the step you rushed.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next three weeks. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Write the strategy of your product area in the kernel format: diagnosis, guiding policy (including what is declined) and coherent actions. Show it to your manager and ask what is wrong. If you cannot say what is declined, note it.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Size one opportunity you could pursue with two routes and a reality check, and write the range, the swing assumption and a cheap test of it.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Run the unit economics of your product or a feature: contribution per unit, payback, lifetime and two sensitivities. Share it with finance or data science for a sanity check.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Write a one-page competitive analysis by customer value: the alternatives customers actually consider (including doing nothing), the positioning map, what each cannot do and likely responses. Test it with sales or support.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Take one claimed advantage in your area (network effects, data, scale) and run it through the sources-of-advantage test.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> Rewrite your team's roadmap or goals (OKRs) so that each item traces to the guiding policy, and flag items that do not. Present the cuts you would make.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the kernel strategy and the declined list (a time you made a strategic choice explicit), the unit-economics and sizing work (a time you quantified an opportunity honestly), and the advantage test (a time you challenged a claim with a mechanism). Keep the numbers real, and keep what you did separate from what the team did.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c18-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Strategy is a diagnosis, a guiding policy and coherent actions; its distinguishing feature is choice, including what you decline. A goal or a project list is not a strategy.</li>\n<li>Size opportunities with two routes and a reality check, separate total from obtainable, and say which assumption swings the answer and how to test it.</li>\n<li>Analyze competition by customer value and positioning, include substitutes and doing nothing, and anticipate the response; test every claimed advantage by its mechanism, durability and copyability.</li>\n<li>Ground recommendations in unit economics with two sensitivities, net out cannibalization, and compare options over the right horizon (build, buy and partner rank differently at three and five years).</li>\n<li>Commit to a recommendation, sequence it with a pilot and a stop rule, and say what would change your mind; use goals frameworks to measure the strategy, and watch for strategy theater.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c18-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Richard P. Rumelt, <em>Good Strategy/Bad Strategy</em> (2011). The kernel and the four hallmarks of bad strategy <code>[V: Rumelt, Good Strategy Bad Strategy, 2011]</code>.</li>\n<li>Michael E. Porter, &quot;What Is Strategy?&quot; <em>Harvard Business Review</em> 74(6), November to December 1996, pp. 61 to 78 <code>[V: HBR 74(6), November to December 1996, pp. 61 to 78]</code>.</li>\n<li>Hamilton Helmer, <em>7 Powers</em> (2016). A rigorous treatment of sources of durable advantage <code>[V: Deep Strategy, 25 October 2016, ISBN 9780998116310]</code>.</li>\n<li>A. G. Lafley and Roger L. Martin, <em>Playing to Win</em> (2013). Where to play and how to win <code>[V: Harvard Business Review Press, 5 February 2013, ISBN 9781422187395]</code>.</li>\n<li>Carl Shapiro and Hal R. Varian, <em>Information Rules</em> (1999), on switching costs, lock-in and network effects <code>[V: Harvard Business School Press, 1999, ISBN 087584863X]</code>.</li>\n<li>John Doerr, <em>Measure What Matters</em> (2018), on OKRs, with the caution that misuse is common <code>[V: Portfolio/Penguin, 24 April 2018, ISBN 9780525536222]</code>.</li>\n<li>Jeff Barr, &quot;Amazon S3&quot; (14 March 2006) and &quot;Amazon EC2 Beta&quot; (25 August 2006); Sébastien Stormacq, &quot;Twenty years of Amazon S3 and building what's next,&quot; AWS News Blog (13 March 2026); all on aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws. Amazon's 2015 annual report on Form 10-K (SEC) for the segment disclosure. The sources for CS-C18-02.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c19\"></span>\n<h2>C19 — Platforms and Ecosystems</h2>\n<span id=\"c19-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;How would you grow a marketplace from nothing?&quot; &quot;You run a payments API; developers say integration is painful. What do you do?&quot; &quot;Design the review policy for an app store.&quot; &quot;Your biggest developer is building a competing product. What now?&quot; Platform questions combine strategy (C18), product craft and some technical fluency, and they are a favorite at companies that run platforms <code>Inferred</code>, because a platform PM's decisions affect people who are not on the team: developers, sellers, buyers and regulators. The interviewer wants to see whether you can name the participants and what each needs, explain why the platform becomes more valuable (or fails to start), set rules that balance speed, safety and trust, and see the risks that come from depending on, and competing with, your own ecosystem.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: platform, marketplace and developer-product questions at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Stripe and others, frequently in strategy or product-sense rounds, and for technical-leaning roles in API-product depth <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. On the job it is the work of PMs on marketplaces, app stores, payment and cloud platforms, and internal platforms.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Whether you reason about more than one side at a time <code>Inferred</code>. Interviewers listen for a clear map of participants and the value each gets; an understanding of network effects with their limits (multi-homing, local networks); a credible plan for the cold start; API and developer-experience thinking; governance that names the tradeoffs and the economics (take rates); and a scan of ecosystem risks.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You map the platform's sides, what each gives and gets, and what links them.</li>\n<li>You distinguish a two-sided market from a developer platform, and say which problems each brings.</li>\n<li>You treat network effects as a mechanism with limits, and plan the cold start with a measure of liquidity.</li>\n<li>You treat an API as a product and measure developer experience, for example the time to a first successful call.</li>\n<li>You set governance (rules, review, take rate, openness) as tradeoffs with consequences for each side.</li>\n<li>You scan for ecosystem risk: dependency, disintermediation, conflict with participants, and regulatory attention.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end platform answers: naming &quot;network effects&quot; as a label, designing for one side only, a launch plan that spreads supply thin, an API treated as plumbing, rules set without thinking about developer trust, and ignoring what happens when the platform competes with its participants.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c19-c19-1-what-a-platform-is-two-sided-markets-versus-developer-\"></span>\n<h4>C19.1 What a platform is: two-sided markets versus developer platforms</h4>\n<p>A <strong>platform</strong> is a product that others build on or transact through, creating value for more than one group of participants. Two broad kinds are common, and many are both. FW-C19-01 is the map and FW-C19-02 chooses the type.</p>\n<p>A <strong>two-sided market</strong> is a market with two distinct groups of participants who need each other, where the platform's value to each group depends on the size and quality of the other. A ride-hailing service (riders and drivers), a marketplace (buyers and sellers) and a job board (employers and candidates) are examples. The platform's job is matching and trust: finding the right counterpart, handling payment, and resolving disputes.</p>\n<p>A <strong>developer platform</strong> is one that others build products on, through interfaces such as APIs (C21), software kits, and tools. An operating system, a cloud service and a payments API are examples. The platform's job is to be a reliable, well-documented foundation, and its participants build things the platform could not, which makes the whole more valuable.</p>\n<p>Many platforms combine both: an app store has developers who build apps (a developer platform) and users who buy them (a market). Name the sides explicitly and ask, for each: what do they give the platform and each other, what do they get, what can they do instead, and what do they fear?</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use the platform lens.</strong> A product with many users but no interaction among them, and no outside builders, is not a platform. Calling it one imports the wrong problems (and hides the real ones).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Asked to grow a marketplace for local services, a candidate says: &quot;It has network effects, so growth will compound. I'd launch in all major cities and spend on marketing.&quot; The interviewer asks what happens in a city with ten sellers and a thousand buyers. The candidate had not considered that a request in such a city rarely gets a good response, so buyers leave and do not return. The network effect is local and conditional, and a launch that spreads supply thinly starts nothing (C19.2).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risks.</strong> Two. <em>One-sided design:</em> optimizing for buyers (low prices, easy cancellation) can drive sellers out, and the reverse. <em>Platform thinking where there is no platform:</em> labeling an ordinary product a platform to sound strategic.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-c19-2-network-effects-flywheels-and-the-cold-start\"></span>\n<h4>C19.2 Network effects, flywheels and the cold start</h4>\n<p>C18.5 defined a network effect. For platforms there are two kinds. A <em>same-side</em> effect: more users of one kind make the platform better for the same kind (a messaging app: more friends). A <em>cross-side</em> effect: more of one side makes the platform better for the other (more drivers shorten rider waits, and more riders raise driver earnings). A <strong>flywheel</strong> is a reinforcing loop in which growth on one side feeds growth on the other; the PM's job is to find where it starts and what slows it.</p>\n<p><strong>Limits.</strong> Network effects are often local (riders in one city do not benefit from drivers in another), can saturate (the thousandth seller adds little), and can be weakened by <strong>multi-homing</strong>, which is when participants use several platforms at once. If drivers can run two ride apps and buyers can check two marketplaces, no platform locks in the market, and competition turns on price, service and incentives. Check, for any claimed effect, how local it is, where it saturates, and whether participants multi-home.</p>\n<p><strong>The cold start.</strong> The <strong>cold-start problem</strong> is the difficulty a platform faces when its value depends on participants who will not join until the platform is already valuable. Tactics (FW-C19-03):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Make it useful to one side alone</em> (&quot;single-player mode&quot;): a tool people use without the other side, so they arrive before the network does.</li>\n<li><em>Narrow the market</em> to one city, one category or one community, so that density is reachable.</li>\n<li><em>Seed the harder side</em>: pay, recruit or build supply yourself, and subsidize if needed.</li>\n<li><em>Borrow an existing network</em> (invite from a base, integrate with one) rather than starting empty.</li>\n<li><em>Measure liquidity</em>, not sign-ups.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Liquidity and density.</strong> Suppose each seller can fulfill a given request with probability 4% (invented). The chance that a buyer's request is matched with at least one of N sellers is 1 − 0.96ᴺ: 56% with 20 sellers, 71% with 30, 91% with 60, 96% with 80. Spreading 160 sellers over 8 cities (20 each) gives 56% matching everywhere; putting them in 2 cities (80 each) gives 96%. Density per market, not the total, decides whether buyers get what they came for and return. The practical rule: launch where you can reach the density threshold, prove it, then expand.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> The arithmetic of matching shows why a narrow launch beats a broad one. <strong>When not to apply it.</strong> For pure developer platforms, where the cold start is about developer adoption (C19.3), not matching.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-c19-3-apis-as-product-developer-experience\"></span>\n<h4>C19.3 APIs as product; developer experience</h4>\n<p>For a developer platform, the API (the defined way programs talk to the platform, C21) is the product, and developers are the users. <strong>Developer experience</strong> is the sum of what it takes for a developer to discover, understand, integrate and succeed with a platform: documentation, examples, software kits, error messages, tooling, support and pricing. A platform with a powerful API and poor developer experience loses developers to one that is easier to start with.</p>\n<p><strong>Measure the first mile.</strong> The key measure is the <em>time to first successful call</em>: from signing up to the first working request. Break it into a funnel (FW-C19-04): discover, sign up, get credentials, make a first call, ship to production. Suppose 10,000 developers sign up (invented); 7,600 get credentials (76%), 5,320 make a first successful call (70% of those), and 3,000 reach production (56% of those): 30% overall. If better documentation and clearer errors raise the three steps to 82%, 85% and 72%, the final count is 10,000 × 0.82 × 0.85 × 0.72 = 5,018, or 50%. Each step is a place to look for friction: unclear sign-up, confusing authentication, a first example that fails, an error that gives no hint.</p>\n<p><strong>Design for stability.</strong> Developers build on your interface, and changes can break their products. Treat compatibility as a feature: version the API, give long notice and support for old versions, and publish a deprecation policy. A platform that keeps old code working earns trust (the S3 case in C18 is an example), and one that breaks developers loses them.</p>\n<p><strong>Other levers.</strong> Sandboxes to try without risk; examples in several languages; clear rate limits and pricing; a status page; a community and support route; analytics for developers on their own usage. The PM owns the developer's journey, reads support tickets and forum posts, and sits with developers during first integrations.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Treating the first integration as the product finds the friction that decides adoption. <strong>When not to invest first.</strong> In a platform with few developers, invest in understanding the first ten, not in tooling for thousands.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-c19-4-governance-rules-quality-take-rates-openness-tradeoffs\"></span>\n<h4>C19.4 Governance: rules, quality, take rates, openness tradeoffs</h4>\n<p>A platform sets the rules for its participants, and the rules are product decisions with consequences for every side. FW-C19-05 is the dial.</p>\n<p><strong>Rules and review.</strong> What may be sold or published, what quality bar applies, how problems are handled. A stricter review protects users (safety, privacy, quality) and slows developers and may deter them; a lighter one speeds releases and raises the risk of harm. The tradeoff is among <em>speed</em> (time to approval), <em>safety</em> (harm prevented) and <em>developer trust</em> (predictability, fairness, a route to appeal). Design for all three: clear written rules, consistent decisions, fast turnaround on routine submissions, human review for hard cases, explanations for rejections, and an appeals path.</p>\n<p><strong>The take rate.</strong> The <strong>take rate</strong> is the share of transaction value a platform keeps. Too high and participants leave, raise prices or bypass the platform; too low and the platform cannot fund itself, its quality and its safety. Take rates vary widely by platform and category <code>[V: Apple and Google app-store fee tiers]</code>. Evaluate with a simple model: if raising the rate lowers the value of transactions through the platform (fewer listings, higher prices, more bypass), revenue may fall. Take an example (invented): at a 30% take rate, transaction value is 100 and revenue 30; at 20%, more developers and lower prices lift transaction value to 108 and revenue to 21.6; at 15%, value 112 and revenue 16.8. Revenue falls as the rate falls, so a cut must be justified by ecosystem health, regulation or competition, not revenue alone. The numbers are assumptions; the discipline is to state how transaction value responds and what each rate is for. Tiered rates (lower for small participants, or for those who use more platform services) are common.</p>\n<p><strong>Openness.</strong> How open should the platform be? Open (anyone can build, sideload, interoperate) maximizes innovation and participation and increases fragmentation, security risk and quality variance. Closed (curated, controlled) gives consistency and safety and invites criticism and regulatory attention. Many platforms choose a middle path with compatibility requirements, certification or tiers of access. The right point depends on the stakes of harm and on how much the platform's value rests on consistency.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Naming the three-way tradeoff and the economics lets you defend a policy. <strong>When not to tighten.</strong> Where harm is low and speed matters, a lighter review with after-the-fact enforcement may serve better.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-c19-5-ecosystem-risk-dependency-disintermediation-regulatory\"></span>\n<h4>C19.5 Ecosystem risk: dependency, disintermediation, regulatory attention</h4>\n<p>Platforms face a set of recurring risks (FW-C19-06):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dependency, in both directions.</strong> Participants depend on the platform (a policy change can end a business), and the platform depends on a few large participants (if its top developers leave, users follow). Concentration is a measure: what share of revenue or usage comes from the top 10?</li>\n<li><strong>Disintermediation.</strong> <strong>Disintermediation</strong> is participants bypassing the platform to transact directly after being matched, which reduces the platform's revenue and its data. It is likely where the platform's value is mostly the introduction (a one-time match) and repeat transactions are easy to do off-platform. Defenses: provide value that continues (payments, protection, insurance, scheduling, reviews), and make staying cheaper than leaving.</li>\n<li><strong>Conflict with participants.</strong> When a platform offers its own products alongside participants' (own-brand goods, first-party apps), participants worry about unfair treatment, especially use of their data and placement. Trust requires clear rules and, often, structural safeguards.</li>\n<li><strong>Regulatory attention.</strong> Large platforms draw scrutiny on fees, openness, competition and data. Rules differ by country and change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>. A PM should know where the platform is exposed and design with that in mind.</li>\n<li><strong>Security and quality incidents.</strong> One bad actor can harm users and the whole platform's reputation. Review, monitoring and fast takedown matter.</li>\n<li><strong>Multi-homing and substitution.</strong> If participants can use rivals easily, retention rests on service quality and incentives.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Regional Lens: India.</strong> Open networks and shared payment rails change platform competition in India. The Unified Payments Interface (C09) lets payments move across many apps, and government-backed open-network initiatives for commerce aim to let buyers and sellers on different apps transact with each other (ONDC is the best known; the trade ministry's DPIIT launched it in April 2022) <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Reuters, 29 April 2022]</code>. The consequence for a platform PM is that lock-in through payments or a closed network is weaker, and competition turns more on service quality, trust and price. Check the current state of any initiative before relying on it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c19-c19-6-platform-metrics\"></span>\n<h4>C19.6 Platform metrics</h4>\n<p>Metrics for a platform must cover each side and the interaction between them (FW-C19-07), with guardrails:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Supply</td>\n<td>Active sellers or developers; new supply per week; supply quality</td>\n<td>Without supply there is nothing to offer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Demand</td>\n<td>Active buyers or users; new demand; repeat rate</td>\n<td>Without demand supply leaves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interaction (liquidity)</td>\n<td>Match rate; time to match; fill rate; conversion from search to transaction</td>\n<td>The platform's core value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quality and trust</td>\n<td>Ratings; disputes per 1,000 transactions; fraud rate; safety incidents</td>\n<td>Trust sustains participation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Economics</td>\n<td>Transaction value; take rate; contribution margin per transaction; payback</td>\n<td>The business must work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Developer experience</td>\n<td>Time to first call; share reaching production; API error rate; support tickets per developer</td>\n<td>Developer adoption</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ecosystem health</td>\n<td>Concentration of the top 10 participants; multi-homing; churn of supply; disintermediation proxies</td>\n<td>Resilience</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Choose a primary metric that reflects the platform's core value (often a measure of successful transactions), and watch the others as guardrails so that growth on one side does not hide trouble on another. A single metric such as gross transaction value can rise while quality, trust and developer satisfaction fall (C05, Goodhart).</p>\n<span id=\"c19-c19-7-case-a-mobile-app-store-as-a-platform\"></span>\n<h4>C19.7 Case: a mobile app store as a platform</h4>\n<p>Section 10 contains a reconstructed case of an app store, built from public documents: the sides (developers and users), the rules, the review process, the revenue share and how it changed, and the risks. It is labeled <code>Reconstructed</code> because the analysis is an inference from public information and not from internal data.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-c19-8-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h4>C19.8 Answer architecture</h4>\n";
